# The Semgrep job's tool.
#
# pip-compile turns this into semgrep.txt: every package, exact versions AND hashes, installed with
# `--require-hashes`. Dependabot keeps it current by re-running pip-compile on the Python its
# header names (3.11), so a bump never pins a set that cannot be installed together. Edit
# THIS file, then regenerate (run in python:3.11-slim):
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --strip-extras --unsafe-package=mcp \
#     --output-file=semgrep.txt semgrep.in
# then semgrep-mcp.txt from semgrep-mcp.in (its header says how).
#
# mcp is left out of semgrep.txt (--unsafe-package) and pinned on its own in semgrep-mcp.txt.
# semgrep 1.179.0, and 1.181.0 as well, declares mcp==1.29.0 exactly, and 1.29.0 has
# GHSA-84m7-p3x7-pcfv (StreamableHTTP sessions without a bound; fixed in 1.30.0), so no set that
# honours semgrep's pin is safe. mcp 1.30.0 declares the same dependencies as 1.29.0, all of them
# in semgrep.txt; semgrep-mcp.txt repeats them at semgrep.txt's versions (its -c line holds them
# there), so the two files together are the whole closure. The job installs both with --no-deps,
# hash-checked, and then runs pip check, which may report only semgrep's own mcp pin against the
# version semgrep-mcp.txt installs; a package either file lacks still fails the job, by name.
# semgrep scan runs on mcp 1.30.0: it imports mcp at startup for its MCP server, which a scan never
# serves. Dependabot keeps --unsafe-package (it reads it back from the header) and updates
# semgrep-mcp.txt on its own.
#
# UNDO when a semgrep release allows mcp>=1.30.0: drop --unsafe-package and semgrep-mcp.in/.txt,
# install semgrep.txt alone without --no-deps, and remove the job's pip-check step.
#
# The same split was made once before, for PyJWT (semgrep 1.178.0 pinned a vulnerable ~=2.13.0),
# and undone when semgrep 1.179.0 allowed pyjwt>=2.15.0.
semgrep
